  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Determine the **largest** interval on which the function $f(x)=-3{e}^{2x}+5$ is concave down.\n\nThe largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave down is\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f(x)$ is concave down on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{\u2033}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=-3{e}^{2x}+5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-6{e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-12{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave down, we solve the inequality ${f}^{\u2033}(x)<0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12{e}^{2x} & <0 \\\\ {e}^{2x} & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave down on $(\\,-\\infty ,\\infty \\,).$",
